so during my interview on yesterday's  show with  coleman hughes this topic of fixing  policing came up and we talked about a  couple of different ideas related to  that and one of the things that i had  mentioned was focusing in a little bit  on traffic enforcement as a sort of  proxy to fighting  a lot of what's wrong with policing in  the united states and i got a bunch of  emails about it from people saying  how would that what would that look like  can you talk about that a little more  so let's let's do that today now as a  pretext to this  i don't have any misconception that  dealing with  traffic laws is the solution to every  problem with policing in the united  states it's not  dealing with traffic enforcement  wouldn't do anything about lack of  training or improper training or the  wrong training  it wouldn't address qualified immunity  if that's something we want to look at  it wouldn't  you know deal with funding or a whole  bunch of other things but there's a  reason why  traffic enforcement is arguably some of  the lowest hanging fruit that we should  look at right away when it comes to  policing which is that  traffic enforcement is number one an  area where police are allowed  a lot of discretion and inherent bias  manifests when there is discretion and  number two traffic enforcement and  traffic related  uh infractions are the primary way in  which  americans interact with police by number  of incidents every single year and so  what what i mean by this is  when it comes to traffic enforcement  we're all familiar with situations  in which i get pulled over and you get  pulled over  and are told we were driving 12 miles an  hour over the speed limit in other words  the pretext for the stop is exactly the  same  but there might be very different  outcomes based on the discretion of the  officer so  i might end up with a speeding ticket  and points on my  insurance paying a higher insurance rate  for several years  i might get my car searched because the  officer  believes that they smell alcohol in my  car when they pull me over  meanwhile you might get pulled over  doing the same  alleged 12 miles an hour over the speed  limit and you get a verbal warning and  have a nice day and the repercussions of  the discretion that is applied  by officers to the same scenario  can be dramatically different even when  the facts are essentially just  the same and this could be just a result  of the bias of the police officer  officers have a lot of discretion in  terms of who they pull over  when they see somebody run a stop sign  for example  once they pull them over who do they  question more aggressively  whose vehicles are they more interested  in searching  who do they have step out of the vehicle  for a field sobriety test when a car  drives by with a tail light out  that discretion can be the difference  between an interaction that leads to a  police shooting  or ultimately an arrest versus someone  who just  drives home not even realizing that they  drove by a police officer with their  taillight  taillight out so because traffic  enforcement is the most common way that  people come into contact with police  every year year in and year out  and it can serve as a sort of blank  slate from which investigations can  start  we should be looking at this very  closely so what are some ways  to reduce the discretion which can be  influenced by bias  when it comes to traffic enforcement one  idea  is to replace some traffic enforcement  with  automation whenever possible by that we  we mostly mean  cameras because of course that is a way  to eliminate  a bias from an individual officer now  you can still choose to put the cameras  in certain neighborhoods and not in  others  and you may have people who are of a  certain economic class  who can better lobby to prevent the  cameras from coming into their  neighborhoods and push the cameras  into poor neighborhoods or black  neighborhoods or whatever the case may  be  but it's something to look at you can  have situations you know on highways  it's typical that on highways people  everybody's driving over the speed limit  then you need to figure out if you're  going to replace highway  speed traps with cameras well how much  speeding is acceptable there are some  complexities there but at least in some  cases  automating elements of traffic  enforcement  would dramatically reduce interactions  with police and it would therefore  reduce the number of interactions that  become  problematic replacing traffic  enforcement  and speed traps with police cameras  would limit  the bias of an officer in leading to  discretion being used in a  discriminatory way  for example now it's not a replacement  for fixing all of the other things that  are broken certainly  but it would be significant nonetheless  so then there's the idea  of having someone other than armed  police  handle some traffic enforcement we've  talked about  homelessness mental illness do we really  need armed police handling  calls to 9-1-1 related to homelessness  it's probably better suited to some  other kind of professional  if we can't have better trained officers  trained like social  workers for example maybe  there would be some other type of  professional that would be better  suited here something analogous could be  you know  for certain types of traffic infractions  you would have something other than an  armed police officer now the devil is in  the details of course  and it's true that while the vast  majority of traffic infractions  don't escalate in any way and the ones  that do sometimes escalate because of  the officer  there can be traffic infractions where  someone knows that they have a warrant  and it can lead to erratic behavior  there's a lot of guns floating around  there so  you've got you've got to think it  through right there's this is not an  obvious and easy  solution but it's something to to look  at but the point is and this is why i  feel strongly about it  when 20 million motorists per year  are pulled over according to the  stanford policing project and these  interactions traffic related  interactions  open the door to such a huge amount of  discretion on the part of the officer  it's an area of low-hanging fruit where  finding some aspects to automate  finding other areas better suited for a  different type of professional than an  armed police officer  we could make a lot of progress there  while i don't want to  ignore this while we look at dealing  with  training qualified immunity lack of a  bad actors database at the federal level  all of the different things that we've  been talking about  let me know your thoughts does this seem  like an avenue that should be pursued  what do you see as the obstacles what do  you see as the possible  shortcomings of it that maybe i'm  ignoring let me know what you think
 

